# converted from 05-the-long-winter.md
@title The Long Winter
@bg mire
@mood uneasy

narrate He went north four years later.
narrate In between, he lived in the Mire, and the Mire was full of people who had been left.
narrate That was the thing nobody in the Order ever understood, and it was the thing that made the Coven. Not a cult. Not a conspiracy. A queue. Every road out of the Mire had a rope across it and a man behind it with a ledger, and behind the rope there were forty thousand people, and every one of them had stood at that rope at some point and been told denied.
narrate Cael did not recruit them. He did not have to. He had simply been there the longest, and he was the only one who had been inside a village that burned, and when people are frightened they go to whoever looks calm.
narrate By the second year there were thirty of them, living in the drowned villages north of the Hollow, and they called themselves nothing in particular, and eventually someone called them the Coven and it stuck.
narrate He was good at it.
narrate That surprised him more than anything else in his life. He had been a competent hedge healer and a decent father and a mediocre gardener, and it turned out that what he was actually for was this: sitting with frightened people and telling them the truth slowly.
narrate He never lied to them. That was the rule. He had made himself a promise about lies in the reeds outside his burning house, and he kept it — which meant that when someone asked him whether the Order would ever open the roads, he said no, and when someone asked whether the Fever would come back, he said yes, and when someone asked whether there was any point in any of it he said not really, but there's a point in the next hour, and that's the one we're working on.
narrate They stayed. He never understood why. He understood it later: he was the first person in their lives who had not managed their feelings for them.
narrate The first unforgivable thing happened in the third year.
narrate A village called Ashen Reach sent for him. They had the Fever — four cases — and they had heard there was a man in the north who knew things.
narrate He went.
narrate And he stood in their shrine and looked at four people he could have saved, because by then he knew the road to the hole in the field the way he knew his own hands, and he knew exactly what it would cost, and he had the ledger in his coat and the cushion under his arm and he had four years of currency in him.
narrate He could have saved all four.
corin neutral I can't.
narrate That was the first lie he told after making the promise. He told it in a shrine in Ashen Reach to a woman holding her brother's hand, and it came out of him easily, smoothly, in the voice of a physician, and he heard himself do it and felt nothing at all.
narrate He walked out and the village burned nine days later, because the Order's arithmetic is the Order's arithmetic, and he heard about it in the spring.
narrate And what he felt, when he heard, was not guilt.
narrate It was confirmation.
narrate He went north in the fourth year.
narrate He would say, later, that he went to ask for the Fever to be ended. He said it to Corin, in a field, and he said it plainly and he believed it when he said it.
narrate He was not lying. That was the problem. He was not lying, and it was not true.
narrate He stood at the edge of the hole in the field, four years after his daughter died and four years after he decided not to feel anything, and he said:
bryn neutral I want to know.
narrate And the deep place, which does not care what you want and only cares what you ask for, gave him what he asked for.
narrate It gave him the shape of the thing. Not words — a knowing, arriving the way a cold arrives, that told him what the Fever was and where it came from and what it would take to end it, and it was all one price and it was payable and it was possible.
narrate He stood there for a long time.
narrate He had come to ask for the ending of the Grey Fever. He had come, in his own mind, as a penitent — a man who had once been selfish and had come back to be selfless, to spend everything, to buy back the thing he should have bought the first time.
narrate And standing there, with the knowledge in him, he did what he had done four years earlier, and what he would do every time for the rest of his life:
narrate He did the arithmetic.
narrate He had four years left of her. Four years out of nine. The first three were already gone — traded for three days — and what remained was the last four, and if he paid for the Fever he would lose those too, and he would not remember her voice or her laugh or the cushion or the river, and he would be a man who had saved forty thousand people and could not have told you why.
narrate He stood at the hole in the field for two hours.
narrate And then he said, out loud, in a voice that did not shake:
corin neutral Then I want the power to do it later. When I'm ready.
narrate That was the second bargain, and it was worse than the first.
narrate The first one he had made for a dying child. The second one he made for himself — so that he would not have to choose, yet, and could tell himself he was choosing.
narrate The deep place gave him what he asked for. It always did.
narrate And the cold came, and the quietness, and something was tidied, and when Cael opened his fist in the grey morning there was a mark on his palm that had been there for four years and was now, in a way he did not fully understand until much later, finished.
narrate He was forty-nine years old.
narrate He had just become the thing that would burn the Hollow, and the only person in the world who knew it was him.
